---
term: Vanguard interest
aka:
  - The Vanguard Interest
section: premise
order: 20
definition: The weakness of centralized movements whose vanguard is prone to internal politics, factionalism, corruption, and other personal weaknesses.
seeAlso:
  - social-charter
  - question-of-arbitration
  - delegation-of-functions
---
Centralizing a movement answers the [[question of arbitration]], because a leadership can set an agenda. The leaders then have interests of their own, however. Internal politics, factional rivalry, and corruption pull the movement away from the interest it exists to serve, and members have little power to correct it.

The Social Charter keeps the benefits of central execution and removes the discretion that invites these weaknesses. Members set the objective. A known, auditable mechanism or a replaceable [[executive agent]] carries it out, and no function judges its own work. See [[delegation of functions]].
